<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1202–<a href="/years/1237">1237</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Sword Brothers, Order of the Sword Brothers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Livonian Brothers of the Sword were a military order founded in 1202 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Albert of Riga</a> to conquer pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. Over two decades they subdued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalians</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>. After a heavy defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a> at Saule in 1236, the survivors were absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1237 as its Livonian branch.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword.jpg" alt="Livonian Brothers of the Sword" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LivoniaKnight.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The order, formally the Militia of Christ of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, was a Catholic order of warrior monks, mostly from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Albert, the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Riga, established it in 1202, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> sanctioned it in 1204. Albert had realised that a standing army in Livonia would be more useful than crusaders who stayed a short time, since the rewards for secular knights were not enough to keep them. The brothers were to help convert the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalians</a> and Selonians who lived along the trade routes eastward from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Sword Brethren fought their first battle in 1205, ambushing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> returning with booty after the Semigallian duke Viesthard asked for help. They built a line of castles along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a>, and since they had superiority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, the native peoples avoided pitched battles and most fighting consisted of raids. In 1211 they formed the core of an army that invaded southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viljandi">Fellin</a> after a siege. The next year they destroyed Saccalian resistance in a battle in which 2,000 Saccalians fell. They found that winter was the best season for war, when frozen swamps and rivers made movement easy, and by 1218 they had conquered southern Estonia. Courland was conquered and converted in 1230.</p><p>From the start the order tended to ignore its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenno">Wenno</a> advanced north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> without Albert's leave and founded the castles of Segewold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesis">Wenden</a>. When he removed Wickbert, a man loyal to Albert, from Wenden, Wickbert fled to the bishop and killed Wenno with an axe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> was divided between King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-ii-of-denmark">Valdemar II of Denmark</a> and Albert in 1222 in a settlement that displeased the order, which seized Danish land and rebuffed a papal legate. After Albert died in 1229 the legate Bernard of Aulne decided that the order should be suppressed, but the order defeated and captured him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The order followed a rule adapted from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>. Knights had to be of noble birth and take vows of obedience, poverty and celibacy, and the order also included soldiers, artisans and clerics. A general assembly of the knights chose a master, who served for life and chose a council and a military chief for each district castle. The first master was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenno">Wenno</a> von Rohrbach, from 1204 to 1209, and the second, Volkwin Schenk von Winterstein, died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saule">Battle of Saule</a> in 1236. The order kept one third of any new territory and the rest went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. After the defeat at Saule the survivors were absorbed in 1237 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> as its Livonian branch.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Livonian Brothers of the Sword” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pressure from the crusading orders encouraged unification: in 1236 a force of Samogitians and other Lithuanians annihilated the <b>Livonian Brothers of the Sword</b> at the Battle of Saule, the event from which the state is conventionally dated here.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Danes lost the area to the <b>Livonian Brothers of the Sword</b> in 1227 but regained it by the Treaty of Stensby in 1238, and their Duchy of Estonia covered the northern provinces of Harria and Vironia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1237 the Order also absorbed the remnants of the <b>Livonian Brothers of the Sword</b>, shattered by the Lithuanians at the Battle of Saule, and so gained a northern branch in Livonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Livonian Brothers of the Sword</b>, an order of German crusading knights, took Tartu, or Dorpat, the last major centre of Estonian resistance on the mainland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-15">15 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Viljandi, the <b>Livonian Brothers of the Sword</b> and their Liv and Latgalian allies defeated an Estonian army of 6,000 under Lembitu of Sakala.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-21">21 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Near modern Šiauliai in northern Lithuania, Samogitian and Semigallian forces destroyed an army of the <b>Livonian Brothers of the Sword</b> returning from a raid, killing the order's master Volkwin.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-22">22 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
